Traditionally in France, voting has been low-tech: voters self-isolated in a booth, their chosen nominee on a pre-printed sheet of paper and placed it in an envelope.
Once officials have confirmed the voter's identity, he or she puts the envelope on the ballot box and signs the electoral roster.
France's election law quite strictly specifies the procedures.
As of 1988, ballot boxes must be transparent, so that voters and observers can see that there are no envelopes at the start of the ballot inside and that no envelopes are released other than those of the duly numbered and eligible voters.
Candidates can send representatives to see every part of the process. In the evening, volunteers count the votes under strict supervision by following specific procedures.
